  The trick, as I see it, is to train yourself (or your fingers 
:-) to do the right thing in most standard situations.  Then you 
only need to strain your mind (which is tough and draining) in 
new/unusual situations.

  I.e. Get into the habit of doing things the "right" way in as
many situations as possible.  Not only is this less wearing, but
much safer when playing while tired.

  Of course, it requires some thought and effort to 

  a) Work out what is the "one best way" to deal with various 
     situations.  (Or just post here when things go wrong, and
     claim it was unavoidable.  Most follow-ups will be more than
     happy to politely point out errors in your play-style. ;^)

  b) Train yourself into habitually doing things that way.

  c) Accept that playing as safely as possible under all 
     circumstances is harder (sometimes much harder) than
     just playing "naturally."



